    "['no shutdown', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, in Junos operational mode (not configuration mode), the immediate equivalent would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/1\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper uses \"disable\" with \"false\" to enable an interface (equivalent to \"no shutdown\"), while \"disable\" alone would be equivalent to \"shutdown\". The interface naming convention also differs (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernet).', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'activate interfaces ge-1/0/1', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 disable false\\n##\\n\\nAlternatively, in Junos operational mode (not configuration mode), the immediate equivalent would be:\\n##\\nactivate interfaces ge-1/0/1\\n##\\n\\nNote: Juniper uses \"disable\" with \"false\" to enable an interface (equivalent to \"no shutdown\"), while \"disable\" alone would be equivalent to \"shutdown\". The interface naming convention also differs (ge-x/x/x instead of GigabitEthernet).', 'trans_template': 'activate interfaces ge-1/0/1'}]]": 1,
    "['switchport trunk allowed vlan [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 2', 'parent_command': ['set interfaces [parameter1]'], 'root': 'set interfaces [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco command:\\n\\n##\\nset interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 2\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- In Juniper, we specify the VLAN membership under the interface\\'s Ethernet switching configuration\\n- \"ge-1/0/1\" is Juniper\\'s equivalent naming for GigabitEthernet1/0/1\\n- \"unit 0\" is typically used as the default logical unit\\n- \"family ethernet-switching\" indicates we\\'re configuring switching (not routing)\\n- \"vlan members 2\" is Juniper\\'s way of specifying allowed VLANs on a trunk (equivalent to Cisco\\'s \"switchport trunk allowed vlan\")', 'trans_template': 'set interfaces ge-1/0/1 unit 0 family ethernet-switching vlan members 2'}]]": 1,
