    "['ip sla [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'services rpm probe test1', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'services rpm probe test1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ip sla 1` command:\\n\\n##\\nservices rpm probe test1\\n##\\n\\nThe Juniper RPM (Real-time Performance Monitoring) is the equivalent feature to Cisco\\'s IP SLA. The `probe test1` creates a probe with ID \"test1\" (analogous to Cisco\\'s probe number \"1\"). Note that the remaining IP SLA parameters (jitter test configuration) would need additional Juniper configuration statements under this probe.', 'trans_template': 'services rpm probe test1'}]]": 1,
    "['ethernet jitter dest-mac [parameter1] vlan [parameter2]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set services rpm probe test-jitter probe-type ethernet-jitter-dest-mac target-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2', 'parent_command': ['services rpm probe test1'], 'root': 'services rpm probe test1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ethernet jitter` SLA command:\\n\\n##\\nset services rpm probe test-jitter probe-type ethernet-jitter-dest-mac target-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2\\nset services rpm probe test-jitter test test-interval 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper requires separate commands to configure the probe and its parameters\\n2. The MAC address format is changed to Juniper\\'s standard (colon-separated)\\n3. The frequency (10 seconds) is implemented as the test interval\\n4. The probe and test names (\"test-jitter\") are arbitrary - you could choose other descriptive names', 'trans_template': 'set services rpm probe test-jitter probe-type ethernet-jitter-dest-mac target-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2'}, {'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set services rpm probe test-jitter test test-interval 10', 'parent_command': ['services rpm probe test1'], 'root': 'services rpm probe test1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': 'Here\\'s the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `ethernet jitter` SLA command:\\n\\n##\\nset services rpm probe test-jitter probe-type ethernet-jitter-dest-mac target-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2\\nset services rpm probe test-jitter test test-interval 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper requires separate commands to configure the probe and its parameters\\n2. The MAC address format is changed to Juniper\\'s standard (colon-separated)\\n3. The frequency (10 seconds) is implemented as the test interval\\n4. The probe and test names (\"test-jitter\") are arbitrary - you could choose other descriptive names', 'trans_template': 'set services rpm probe test-jitter test test-interval 10'}]]": 1,
    "['frequency [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'set services rpm probe test probe-type ethernet-jitter test test-name test1 jitter-destination-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2 probe-count 1 probe-interval 10', 'parent_command': ['services rpm probe test1', 'set services rpm probe test-jitter probe-type ethernet-jitter-dest-mac target-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2'], 'root': 'services rpm probe test1', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here's the Juniper equivalent of the Cisco `frequency 10` command within the IP SLA context:\\n\\n##\\nset services rpm probe test probe-type ethernet-jitter test test-name test1 jitter-destination-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2 probe-count 1 probe-interval 10\\n##\\n\\nNote: \\n1. Juniper uses RPM (Real-Time Performance Monitoring) instead of IP SLA\\n2. The `probe-interval 10` parameter is the equivalent of Cisco's `frequency 10` (seconds between probes)\\n3. The entire probe configuration needs to be specified in Juniper as it's more structured than Cisco's incremental configuration\\n4. I included the MAC address and VLAN for context, though strictly only the interval parameter was requested\", 'trans_template': 'set services rpm probe test probe-type ethernet-jitter test test-name test1 jitter-destination-mac 00:00:00:00:00:02 vlan-id 2 probe-count 1 probe-interval 10'}]]": 1,
